 Capabilities are project-defined (for example a runtime, compiler, editor or inspection tool). Verify required access before dependent work. Attempt bounded recovery automatically. A missing mandatory capability is BLOCKED; continue independent work and preserve the blocker without asking the user to do QA. No simulated capability is an operational integration claim.
 
 Lead implements and runs relevant deterministic checks. Reviewer independently chooses goal scenarios and observes actual execution before reading implementation explanations. Then it inspects the exact diff, relevant source, safety/state transitions and test quality. Findings give severity, location, trigger, observed/expected result, impact and fix. Keep one concise handoff, not the whole conversation.
+
+For checks expected to produce verbose output, capture complete stdout and stderr into uniquely named files under ignored `.cache/.runtime/` before tool output enters the conversation. Preserve the original command, arguments, working directory and original process exit status; distinguish capture or summary failures from command failure. Display a compact receipt with that command, exit status, warnings, skipped or incomplete checks, failure summary and artifact paths; mark any unassessed field unknown. Read the retained output as needed to establish those fields, diagnose failures and satisfy every required evidence obligation; a zero exit or abbreviated receipt alone never establishes success. If capture is incomplete or unavailable, preserve the blocker and recover with complete ordinary output rather than accepting a truncated result. Use ordinary output for short checks. Keep all required checks, criteria and independent review unchanged. Explicitly provide the Independent Reviewer the actual observation evidence it needs, including relevant raw output; local paths and the lead's summary alone do not establish reviewer access or independent verification.
 
 Each criterion is PASS (observed success), FAIL (observed defect), or BLOCKED (missing or incomplete evidence, including tooling failures). Explicit exclusions are separate and never PASS. Evidence identifies observer, UTC time, source revision or file hashes, command/action, state, expected/actual and artifact location. A numeric score is demonstrated PASS / all frozen applicable criteria; a high score never overrides a blocker. Independent behavior and code verdicts must both be clear where applicable. Acceptance requires all required criteria PASS and no unresolved actionable findings. A build alone proves neither behavior nor whole-product quality.
 
